[Music]  imagine a boat is crossing a river from  one bank to another  it's moving in the direction  perpendicular to the flow of the river  tell me will the boat reach the bank at  this place p  or somewhere to the right of it  that's right the boat will reach the  bank somewhere on the right side of this  point b  we know that the motion of the boat is  also influenced by the velocity of the  river  along with the velocity of the boat's  engine  so the boat actually travels with a  different velocity  which is the resultant of these two  velocities  if vector v1 represents the velocity of  the boat's engine  and vector v2 represents the velocity of  the river  then the boat's velocity v3 will be the  vector sum  of these two velocities and what will be  this vector v3  let's find out let's say this arrow  represents vector v1  the velocity of the boat's engine we  know that the reverse velocity is  perpendicular to v1  so let this arrow represent the reverse  velocity v2  now we know intuitively that the vector  v3  which is the boat's actual velocity will  be somewhere in between these two  vectors  but how can we find it accurately  actually to find the vector v3 precisely  there is a law of vector addition called  the parallelogram law of vector addition  now let's suppose that we have a  parallelogram  and the two adjacent sides of it  represent the two vectors  a and b in magnitude and direction like  this  then the sum of the vectors a and b is  represented  in magnitude and direction by the  diagonal of the parallelogram  through the common point this is known  as the parallelogram law of vector  addition  take a moment and have a look at it  now let's get back to our example can  you apply this law here  to find the boat's actual velocity v3  for this let's draw a line segment  parallel vector v1  here and similarly a line segment  parallel to vector v2 here  so since v1 and v2 are perpendicular to  each other  we get a rectangle here now according to  the parallelogram law of vector addition  this diagonal will represent the vector  v3  we can find the magnitude and the  direction of the vector v3  by using the geometric properties of  this rectangle  so in this way we can apply this law to  find the vector sum  of two vectors now suppose we are given  these two vectors  c and d whose initial points are not the  same  can you find their vector sum for this  we will just have to move one of the  vectors parallelly  without affecting its magnitude and  direction  such that the initial point of both the  vectors coincide  if we move vector d then we will get  this now  we draw two lines parallel to vector c  and d to form a parallelogram  then according to the parallelogram law  of vector addition  this diagonal will represent the sum of  the vectors c  and d but now wait in the previous video  we saw that the sum of two vectors is  given by the triangle law of vector  addition  here according to this law we move  vector d  such that its initial point coincides  with the terminal point like this  so we see that these three points abc  will form a triangle now the third side  of the triangle in the opposite order  than these two  represents the vector sum of these two  vectors  thus we have two laws for vector  addition  which one should we follow to find the  vector sum  actually both the laws give us the same  resultant vector  these two laws are equivalent to each  other  we will see this in the next lesson  here we saw how to add vectors  in the next lesson we will also look at  how to subtract two vectors  to stay updated and to keep learning  such interesting concepts  do subscribe to our channel  you

